Frederick Roeber writes:
>How about:  When in the "move the cursor and then hit '.'" mode, hitting
>other characters like '>', '_', etc. move the cursor to the next character
>on the screen matching it.  `Next' meaning further to the right on that
>line, or on a lower line, or wrapping again from the top.  If there's
>more than one, just hit it again, you'll cycle through them.  When the
>cursor is on the one you want, hit '.'.  This would just be an interface
>change, for teleporting, looking, etc.  All the benefits for slow terminals,
>but no play impact.

Hear, hear!  Of course, this scheme is applicable to *any* character displayed
on the screen... so one could easily hop through the list of all up stairs,
all altars, all large Humanoids, or all wands that the character knows of.

How often have I wanted to teleport right next to a juicy wraith
on the other side of the screen?  It would be so much faster to type
	"^tWj."   than  "^thhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhkkkhh."
Doesn't look *that* difficult to program, either.  Dev team?

-Leonard


